\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Pure gauge means trivial holonomy}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{K} and the affine connection #{g} be those of
\ref{fdip-00OC}. The connection is pure gauge when there is a
vertex assignment #{h} with
##{g_e=h_{h(e)}\,h_{s(e)}^{-1}}
on every oriented edge. By \ref{fdip-006F}, this happens exactly
when the holonomy of every closed path is the identity pair
#{(1,0)}. Equivalently, every attaching walk has identity holonomy,
and the holonomies of a generating set of based loops for the
fundamental group of #{K} are the identity. On a rectangle, the
boundary product of \ref{fdip-0093} is the identity whenever every
plaquette holonomy is the identity.}

\p{Suppose #{R_e=1} on every edge. Path composition then adds the
translational components. The connection is pure gauge exactly when
those components are the coboundary of a vertex cochain #{X} with
values in #{V},
##{a_e=X(h(e))-X(s(e)).}
Equivalently, the sum of #{a} around every square vanishes, and the
sum of #{a} around each cycle in a generating set of
#{H_1(K;\mathbb{Z})} vanishes. A constant gauge #{(S,0)} replaces
#{a} by #{\rho(S)a}. A translation gauge changes #{a} by a
coboundary. In this sector the quotient by the full gauge group is
the quotient of the closed cochains, modulo coboundaries, by the
global action #{[a]\mapsto[\rho(S)a]}.}

\proof{
\p{\ref{fdip-006F} places a connection in the pure-gauge orbit
exactly when every closed-path holonomy is the identity.
\ref{fdip-0093} writes a rectangle boundary as a product of
conjugated plaquette holonomies, so identity plaquettes make that
boundary the identity.}

\p{With every #{R_e=1}, the product rule of \ref{fdip-00OB} gives
#{(1,a)(1,c)=(1,a+c)}, and the inverse of #{(1,a)} is #{(1,-a)}.
The sum of #{a} on the boundary of a square is the translational
holonomy of that square. The sum of #{a} on a cellular boundary
therefore vanishes once every square sum vanishes, and the sum on a
closed path depends only on the class of that path in
#{H_1(K;\mathbb{Z})}. Vanishing on a generating set implies
vanishing on every closed path.}

\p{Choose a spanning tree rooted at a vertex #{o}, as in
\ref{fdip-006F}, and define #{X(o)=0}. Along the tree path from
#{o} to a vertex #{x}, let #{X(x)} be the sum of the forward values
of #{a}, with a sign change on an edge traversed against its
orientation. If a tree edge runs from #{s} to #{h} in the direction
away from the root, then #{X(h)=X(s)+a_e}. If the rooted path
traverses that edge backwards, the inverse contributes #{-a_e} and
the same difference #{a_e=X(h)-X(s)} results. For an edge outside
the tree, the based loop that follows the tree to the source,
crosses the edge and returns along the tree has sum
#{X(s)+a_e-X(h)}. That sum is #{0}, so again
#{a_e=X(h)-X(s)}. Thus #{a} is the coboundary of #{X}. A constant
gauge #{(S,0)} fixes every rotational component that equals #{1}
and replaces #{a_e} by #{\rho(S)a_e}. A gauge #{(1,\xi)} adds
#{\xi(h(e))-\xi(s(e))}.}
}
